'''Naruto Prefecture''' ({{nihongo|鳴門県|Naruto-ken}}) is a prefecture of [[Okaiken]] located in the [[Sanzu Region]] on the island of [[Sanzu]] (三州), occupying the northern portion of the island facing the [[Sanzu Strait]] toward [[Harujima]]. Its population as of 2026 was estimated at 872,787. It is the 35th most populous prefecture and the 33rd largest by land area, spanning approximately 3,450.48 square kilometres. Its capital city is [[Naruto, Naruto|Naruto]].

Naruto Prefecture is known throughout Okaiken for its historic [[shipbuilding]] industry, the spectacular [[Naruto Whirlpools]] in the strait separating Sanzu from Harujima, and the [[Naruto Odori]] — one of the nation's largest and most celebrated summer dance festivals, drawing over one million spectators annually. The prefecture's position at the maritime gateway between Harujima and the Sanzu island chain has shaped its economy and culture for centuries, earning it the nickname "The Forge of the Strait" (海峡の炉, ''Kaikyō no Ro'').

==History==
[[File:Naruto Whirlpools Historical.jpg|thumb|left|250px|19th-century woodblock print depicting the Naruto Whirlpools, the natural phenomenon that gave the prefecture its name.]]

===The Whirlpool Strait and early settlement===
The name "Naruto" (鳴門, literally "roaring gate") derives from the [[Naruto Whirlpools]] (鳴門の渦潮, ''Naruto no Uzushio''), powerful tidal vortexes that form in the narrow strait between Sanzu and Harujima. These whirlpools, among the largest in the world, occur when the tide shifts and massive volumes of water rush through the 1.3-kilometre-wide channel, creating swirling columns up to 20 metres in diameter. The thunderous sound of the whirlpools — audible from miles inland — gave the strait its name and made it a sacred site for the indigenous coastal peoples.

Human settlement in the Naruto region dates back millennia. The sheltered [[Seto Inland Sea]] coastline, with its countless small islands and calm waters, provided ideal conditions for fishing communities and maritime trade. During the Sengoku period, the Naruto strait became a critical chokepoint for naval operations, and the local lords — the [[Naruto clan]] — built a formidable maritime domain by controlling passage through the strait and taxing the shipping that passed through.

===Shipbuilding and the Murakami legacy===
The calm, island-dotted waters of the Seto Inland Sea coast made Naruto a natural center for [[shipbuilding]]. The tradition traces its roots to the [[Murakami suigun]] (村上水軍), the legendary Murakami naval forces that dominated the Inland Sea during the Sengoku period. The Murakami lords established shipyards in the sheltered bays of what is now the [[Okawa]] district, constructing warships and merchant vessels using techniques that would define Naruto's industrial identity for centuries.

Under the Honda Shogunate, the Naruto shipyards transitioned from military production to commercial shipbuilding, constructing the coastal freighters and fishing vessels that connected the Sanzu islands to the Harujima mainland. The tradition of wooden shipbuilding persisted well into the 20th century, and the prefecture remains one of Okaiken's most important centers for both commercial and naval ship construction.

===Modern era===
The Saito Restoration of 1871 formally established Naruto Prefecture on February 8, 1872. The capital was placed at the town of Naruto, which sat at the narrowest point of the strait and served as the primary ferry crossing to Harujima. The completion of the [[Naruto Grand Bridge]] in 1973 — a suspension bridge spanning the whirlpool strait — and the extension of the [[Harujima Shinkansen]] to Naruto Station in 1985 transformed the prefecture from an isolated maritime outpost into a vital transportation hub connecting Harujima to the Sanzu island chain.

==Geography==
[[File:Naruto Strait Bridge.jpg|thumb|right|300px|The Naruto Grand Bridge spanning the whirlpool strait, with the Harujima Shinkansen visible on the lower deck.]]

Naruto Prefecture occupies the northern portion of Sanzu Island, facing the Harujima mainland across the [[Naruto Strait]]. The prefecture spans approximately 3,450.48 km², making it the 33rd largest in Okaiken.

The terrain is dominated by the [[Yoshino River]] (吉野川, ''Yoshino-gawa''), the longest river in Sanzu, which flows eastward through the center of the prefecture before emptying into the Naruto Strait. The river's broad alluvial plain — known as the [[Naruto Plain]] — contains the prefectural capital and the majority of the population. The northern and southern districts are mountainous, with peaks rising to over 1,000 metres, covered in dense subtropical forest.

The western coastline along the [[Seto Inland Sea]] is the prefecture's most distinctive geographic feature. A scattering of small islands — collectively known as the [[Naruto Islands]] (鳴門諸島, ''Naruto-shotō'') — dots the calm waters between the mainland and the open Shendan Ocean. These islands, numbering over forty, range from inhabited communities of several thousand residents to uninhabited rocky outcrops. The Inland Sea coast is characterized by pine-covered beaches, olive groves, and traditional fishing villages, and is often compared to the more famous inland sea landscapes of [[Nakamizu]].

