==Economy==
[[File:Naruto Shipyard.jpg|thumb|right|300px|The Naruto Heavy Industries shipyard on the Seto Inland Sea, one of the largest commercial shipbuilding facilities in Sanzu.]]

Naruto Prefecture's economy is built on three pillars: [[shipbuilding]], [[agriculture]], and [[tourism]]. The prefecture's strategic position at the maritime gateway between Harujima and Sanzu has made it a natural center for ship construction and maritime logistics, while its mild Inland Sea climate supports a thriving citrus and sudachi industry. The [[Naruto Odori]] festival and the [[Naruto Whirlpools]] draw over one million visitors annually, making tourism an increasingly vital sector.
Within the Sanzu regional division, Naruto's industrial footprint is defined by advanced maritime propulsion and strait hydrokinetic energy. Naruto Heavy Industries operates automated drydocks that construct specialized composite-hull patrol craft, high-speed pilot launches, and tidal current turbine generators installed directly within the high-velocity passages of the Naruto Strait. The prefecture's marine cooperatives manage intensive sea-bream and wakame seaweed aquaculture farms that thrive in the nutrient-rich, oxygenated tidal currents, while coastal logistics terminals handle inter-island roll-on/roll-off freight linking northern Sanzu with the western industrial nodes of the Seto Inland Sea.

